  "articleBody": "When it comes to installing a wheelchair accessible vanity, even small oversights can lead to major frustrations. Avoiding these common mistakes will make a big difference in the final usability and safety of your bathroom.\n\n1.  **Using a Standard Vanity Height:** This is perhaps the most frequent error. A standard vanity height (often 36 inches or more) will be too high for a wheelchair user. The counter or rim should be installed at a **34-inch maximum** to allow comfortable access.\n2.  **Forgetting Toe Space:** While knee clearance is often considered, toe space is sometimes overlooked. Without adequate toe clearance (part of the roughly **27 inches high, 30 inches wide, and 19 inches deep** under-sink zone), a wheelchair user can't get close enough to the sink.\n3.  **Leaving Exposed Plumbing Unprotected:** Hot water pipes directly under the sink pose a burn risk. All exposed plumbing must be insulated or otherwise protected to prevent accidental contact.\n4.  **Choosing a Sink That Is Too Deep:** A very deep sink bowl can make reaching the faucet and using the basin difficult, even if the counter height is correct. Look for shallower, accessible-friendly sink designs.\n5.  **Assuming Wall-Mounted Automatically Works:** While wall-mounted vanities often provide more knee clearance, you still need to verify the specific dimensions and ensure sufficient clear floor space. Don't assume it's a ",
